  "slug": "disturbances-eco-07dc",
  "summary": "Three recent models of phage, RNA virus and multispecies competition test when defenses, delays and selection rules permit persistence or extinction.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how thresholds and delays govern whether populations persist, coexist or go extinct. The delay differential equation model of polymerase-mediated RNA virus quasispecies analyzes bifurcations, complementation and robustness, including delay-induced extinction (tau-tipping). The minimal temperate phage competition model identifies when prophage-borne defenses invade, coexist cyclically or persist. The hierarchical May-Leonard study addresses selection rules that determine which species combinations persist while others disappear. What is new is the treatment of persistence and extinction as threshold-governed outcomes across these distinct systems, rather than single-system dynamics. The items are heterogeneous, so the shared element is conceptual and methodological rather than a single common mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-09-14"
}
